## Experiment
Topic: Topic Empirical AI/ML research. Pick one open, concrete question about the behavior of machine-learning models — for example: training dynamics and generalization (grokking, double descent), in-context learning in small transformers, calibration of model confidence, prompt/format sensitivity of language models, tokenization effects, or optimizer behavior — that can be investigated rigorously with small-scale experiments runnable on a single machine (CPU or Apple-Silicon MPS), and study it end-to-end.
Goal: Use a validated modular-addition grokking baseline to test whether an exactly softmax-invisible rank-one output component, decayed through an empirically fitted total-norm threshold, changes paired grokking time.

Protocol to implement:
1. Environment and determinism: implement in Python 3.11 with PyTorch and matplotlib only. Run on CPU to avoid nondeterministic MPS kernels. Set OMP_NUM_THREADS=1, MKL_NUM_THREADS=1, torch.set_num_threads(1), torch.use_deterministic_algorithms(True), and seed Python, NumPy, and PyTorch. Use float32 for training and float64 only for intervention audits and norm calculations. Record package versions and elapsed wall time.
2. Task generator: for modulus p, generate all p^2 ordered pairs (a,b) with a,b in {0,...,p-1}; the label is y=(a+b) mod p. For each seed in {11,22,33,44}, permute the p^2 examples using NumPy Generator(PCG64(seed)); use the first floor(f*p^2) examples as training data and all remaining examples as test data. Inputs are concatenated one-hot vectors [one_hot(a), one_hot(b)] of length 2p. Use full-batch training and never resample examples.
3. Model: use a bias-free MLP Linear(2p,h), ReLU, Linear(h,h), ReLU, Linear(h,p), with PyTorch default Linear initialization after setting the run seed. Call the final weight W, with shape p by h. Compute cross-entropy from centered logits z-z.mean(dim=-1,keepdim=True); this is mathematically equivalent to ordinary softmax but makes the class-common direction explicitly absent from the loss. Define total squared parameter norm S as the sum of squared entries of every effective model parameter, excluding optimizer state.
4. Baseline-validation search: test the following configurations in order and select the first one satisfying the success criteria in Step 6 for all four seeds: A=(p=17,f=0.40,h=128,learning_rate=0.01,weight_decay=1.0,max_steps=40000); B=(p=23,f=0.50,h=128,learning_rate=0.01,weight_decay=1.0,max_steps=40000); C=(p=23,f=0.40,h=256,learning_rate=0.005,weight_decay=1.0,max_steps=50000). Use AdamW with betas=(0.9,0.98), eps=1e-8, the listed learning rate and weight decay, and apply weight decay to every model tensor. Do not use dropout, batching, learning-rate schedules, clipping, or early stopping except as specified below.
5. During each ordinary AdamW run, evaluate train accuracy, train cross-entropy, test accuracy, centered-logit RMS, and total parameter norm every 20 optimizer steps, including step 0. Save model and optimizer states at every evaluation after memorization. Define the memorization step M as the first evaluation starting a sequence of five evaluations for which train accuracy is exactly 1.0 and train cross-entropy is at most 0.001. Define the ordinary grokking step G as the first evaluation starting a sequence of ten evaluations, spanning 200 optimizer steps, for which test accuracy is at least 0.95 at every evaluation. A run may stop after the evaluation at G+180 has confirmed this window.
6. A configuration is a validated grokking baseline only if every one of the four seeds has finite M and G, G-M is at least 200 optimizer steps, and at least one evaluation between M and G has test accuracy below 0.90. If a configuration fails for any seed, discard all results from that configuration and try the next configuration. If no configuration succeeds, label the experiment INVALID_BASELINE and do not report the hypothesis as refuted. This validity outcome is mandatory rather than a hypothesis decision.
7. Fit one norm threshold using only the four successful ordinary runs from the selected configuration. For seed i, collect total norms at the ten evaluations G_i,G_i+20,...,G_i+180 that define sustained grokking. Let T=1.05 times the maximum of these 40 norms. Report T and the 40 underlying values. This is the sole fitted total-norm threshold used in every fork.
8. Select one checkpoint per seed. Starting at M_i, choose the earliest saved evaluation C_i satisfying all of: train accuracy=1.0, train cross-entropy<=0.001, test accuracy<0.90, total norm<T, and C_i<G_i. If no such checkpoint exists for any seed, label the experiment INVALID_CHECKPOINT and do not make a supported/refuted decision. Audit that the selected checkpoint retains its recorded metrics after reloading, with absolute discrepancies below 1e-7 for loss and below 1e-6 for norm and accuracy.
9. Create an unmodified no-decay fork from each C_i by restoring both model and AdamW optimizer state and setting optimizer weight_decay to 0 without changing any other optimizer field. Train for at most 5000 additional steps, evaluate every 20 steps, and compute the fork-relative sustained grokking step G0_i using the same ten-consecutive-evaluation 95% definition. Save the model trajectory needed for audits. If any no-decay fork lacks finite G0_i, label the experiment INVALID_FORK rather than refuting the hypothesis.
10. Construct the rank-one intervention independently for each seed from the same C_i state. Let W be the checkpoint output matrix, let u be the p-vector of ones, and let s=W^T u. Choose the standard basis vector e_j whose orthogonalized residual r_j=e_j-s*(s^T e_j)/(s^T s) has greatest norm; if s^T s<1e-20, use e_0. Set v=r_j/||r_j||. This guarantees <W,u v^T>=0 up to numerical precision. Let S be the checkpoint total squared parameter norm and set alpha_0=sqrt(S/p). Define the effective output matrix as W_eff=W+alpha*u v^T. Because ||alpha_0*u v^T||_F^2=S and the cross term is zero, its total squared parameter norm must equal 2S.
11. Audit the intervention before training on all p^2 examples in float64. Compare the original logits z and intervened logits z'. Require: relative error in total squared norm doubling <=1e-8; max absolute difference between softmax(z) and softmax(z') <=1e-10; max absolute difference between centered logits <=1e-10; max absolute cross-entropy difference <=1e-10; and max absolute prediction-accuracy difference=0. Also require the centered-logit RMS difference <=1e-10. Failure is INVALID_INVARIANCE, not evidence against the hypothesis.
12. Implement the intervention without merging it into W: keep alpha and v separately, compute raw logits as the base output plus alpha*(hidden dot v)*u, and compute training loss from the base centered logits, which are exactly the centered effective logits. Restore the same checkpoint optimizer state as the no-decay fork, set weight_decay=0 for every ordinary parameter, and never optimize v. Thus only the added class-common component receives explicit decay, while the base model follows the same no-decay update rule as the comparison fork.
13. Let D_i=max(20,20*round((0.5*G0_i)/20)). Before optimizer update t, set alpha_t=alpha_0*max(1-t/D_i,0), producing deterministic linear decay of only the added component. Continue with alpha=0 after t>=D_i. Measure the effective total norm every optimizer step in float64 and record the first threshold-crossing step X_i at which the norm changes from >T on the preceding step to <=T. Require finite X_i, X_i<=D_i, and intervention norm>T at step 0. If base-parameter movement causes no crossing, rerun that intervention once with D_i replaced by max(20,floor(D_i/2) rounded to a multiple of 20); if it still does not cross, label the experiment INVALID_CROSSING.
14. Train each intervention fork for the same 5000-step maximum and compute its fork-relative sustained grokking step G1_i using exactly the no-decay definition. At every evaluation compare the base parameters and base centered logits of the paired forks. Report their maximum absolute discrepancies; require checkpoint probability invariance as in Step 11, but do not invalidate later outcome differences because detecting such differences is the purpose of the test. If G1_i is censored while G0_i is finite, assign relative shift infinity and count it as a refutation, provided all baseline, checkpoint, invariance, and crossing checks passed.
15. For each seed compute paired relative grokking shift R_i=abs(G1_i-G0_i)/G0_i. The preregistered primary statistic is R_max=max_i R_i. Support the hypothesis if all validity checks pass, all four intervention forks grok, and R_max<0.10. Refute the hypothesis if all validity checks pass and either R_max>=0.10 or at least one intervention fork fails to grok within 5000 steps while its paired no-decay fork does. Do not substitute calibration failure, absent grokking, failed invariance, missing threshold crossing, or an invalid checkpoint for a refutation.
16. Save results.csv with one row per seed containing configuration, M_i, ordinary G_i, C_i, checkpoint train/test metrics, checkpoint norm, T, doubled intervention norm, invariance errors, G0_i, G1_i, X_i, R_i, and final status. Save summary.json containing R_max, mean and median R_i, all validity flags, selected configuration, elapsed time, and the supported/refuted/invalid decision.
17. Save key_result.png at 1600 by 700 pixels with two panels. Left: paired scatter of G0_i versus G1_i, labeled by seed, with the identity line and dashed lines at slopes 0.9 and 1.1. Right: for the seed with largest R_i, plot test accuracy and effective total norm versus fork-relative step for both forks; include a horizontal 95%-accuracy line, horizontal threshold T, vertical threshold-crossing line X_i, and vertical grokking lines G0_i and G1_i.

Metrics to compute: Ordinary memorization step M: first 20-step evaluation beginning five consecutive evaluations with train accuracy 1.0 and train cross-entropy <=0.001., Sustained grokking step G, G0, or G1: first 20-step evaluation beginning ten consecutive evaluations, spanning 200 optimizer steps, with test accuracy >=0.95., Fitted norm threshold T: 1.05 times the largest total parameter norm in the 40 sustained-grokking evaluations from the four successful ordinary AdamW runs., Norm-doubling error: abs(S_intervention-2*S_checkpoint)/(2*S_checkpoint), required <=1e-8., Softmax-invariance error: maximum absolute probability difference over all p^2 examples and classes, required <=1e-10 in the float64 checkpoint audit., Centered-logit-invariance error: maximum absolute difference after subtracting each example's class-mean logit, required <=1e-10., Threshold-crossing step X: first intervention step whose effective total norm is <=T after being >T on the preceding step., Per-seed relative grokking shift R_i: abs(G1_i-G0_i)/G0_i., Primary decision statistic R_max: maximum R_i across the four matched seeds; hypothesis threshold is 0.10., Paired trajectory discrepancy: maximum absolute difference in base parameters and base centered logits between no-decay and intervention forks at matched evaluations.
Expected if hypothesis holds: All four ordinary runs produce delayed sustained grokking, every intervention exactly doubles squared norm while passing the probability and centered-logit audits, every manipulated norm crosses T, and every paired relative grokking shift is below 0.10, so R_max<0.10; the paired points lie between the 0.9x and 1.1x lines despite visibly different raw total-norm trajectories.
Expected if hypothesis fails: After all validity checks pass and every manipulated norm crosses T, at least one paired relative grokking shift is >=0.10, making R_max>=0.10, or an intervention fork fails to reach sustained 95% test accuracy within 5000 steps while its matched no-decay fork does; baseline failure or failure to create an invariant threshold-crossing intervention is invalid, not a refutation.
Wall-clock budget: about 30 minutes. Keep the experiment inside it; if
something is slow, reduce sample sizes rather than exceeding the budget.

